{"body":{"asks":"What Fable found reading the git reader, still to mend; mended on 2026-11-11 in Mending the edges: a folder that is no repository refused in words, an unborn HEAD read as none, an annotated tag peeled to its commit, a ref and an object each built in one place, git asked in plain words. Left: every read spawns git, so a read costs about 150 ms and a whole minutes, wanting one long-lived cat-file; ref and type ids by name collide across repositories, waiting on a root; a submodule's entry holds what is not there; the tip is HEAD's tree only by order; all() spawns once per step where git log and ls-tree would do in two; a moving ref shows no new version; a tag of a tag may peel one level only on older git, and a lightweight tag on a tree or blob puts what is no commit under commit.","raised":[{"$ref":"1M75NKHCHV1S041"}],"stands":"open"},"id":"1M75NKZW7HX8001","instance":["1M6YBY3G000001E"],"name":"The git reader's faults"}
